  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What does CNC stand for?",
      "a": "CNC stands for computer numerical control. A computer reads a program of numbers and commands and drives the machine's motors to move the tool exactly, without a person turning the handles.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a CNC machine?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is G-code?",
      "a": "G-code is the language most CNC machines understand. Each line is a simple command, like move in a straight line to these coordinates at this speed, or turn the spindle on. Software generates it from the 3D design.",
      "asks": [
        "How is a CNC machine programmed?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does a milling cutter remove metal?",
      "a": "A milling cutter has several hard, sharp edges, often made of tungsten carbide. As it spins, each edge bites into the metal and peels off a small chip, and moving the work past the cutter carves the shape.",
      "asks": [
        "What is milling?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does a CNC machine move so precisely?",
      "a": "Servo motors turn precise ball screws, which convert turning into tiny straight movements. Sensors constantly measure each axis's position, and the controller corrects any error, so the tool lands within about a hundredth of a millimetre.",
      "asks": [
        "How accurate is CNC machining?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why is coolant sprayed on the cutter?",
      "a": "Cutting metal makes a lot of heat, which can blunt the tool and warp the part. Coolant carries heat away, lubricates the cut and flushes chips out of the way.",
      "asks": [
        "What is the liquid sprayed on a CNC machine?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Numerically controlled milling was developed at MIT with John T. Parsons, demonstrated in 1952.",
    "A 3-axis mill moves the tool along X, Y and Z; 5-axis machines can also tilt and rotate the part or tool."
  ],
